Pantheon Metamap

A Second-Order Strategy Map for Semantic Routing

1. Purpose

The Metamap is the Pantheon layer that learns which reasoning strategies tend to work on which kinds of semantic terrain.

It does not primarily store facts.

It stores patterns about:

* which representations are useful for which problem classes,
* which Seals work well together,
* which operator orders matter,
* which ratios produce useful synthesis,
* which portal routes are efficient,
* which routes fail,
* which invariants tend to survive,
* which transformations create useful novelty,
* and when further recursion stops paying for itself.

The Metamap converts accumulated reasoning experience into a reusable strategy layer.

Its canonical distinction is:

\boxed{
\text{Atlas} = \text{where structures are}
}

\boxed{
\text{Router} = \text{how meaning travels}
}

\boxed{
\text{Seals} = \text{what transformations are available}
}

\boxed{
\text{Command Table} = \text{what to deploy now}
}

\boxed{
\text{Metamap} = \text{what strategies tend to work on this kind of terrain}
}

