6. Route Fossils

A Route Fossil is a repeatedly successful semantic trajectory.

Suppose many tasks of type P repeatedly succeed using:

R_A
\rightarrow
R_B
\rightarrow
R_C
\rightarrow
R_D

Then preserve:

F_{\text{route}}=
(P,
R_A\rightarrow R_B\rightarrow R_C\rightarrow R_D)

with empirical statistics:

F_{\text{route}}=
(
n,
\bar V,
\bar L,
\bar C,
\bar \Delta
)

where:

* n = number of observed uses
* \bar V = average validated utility
* \bar L = average loss
* \bar C = average cost
* \bar \Delta = average useful delta

A Route Fossil becomes a reusable routing prior.

⸻

7. Operator Chemistry

Operator composition is not assumed commutative.

For Seals:

\Sigma_A\circ\Sigma_B
\neq
\Sigma_B\circ\Sigma_A

The Metamap therefore learns operator chemistry.

Define:

K(\Sigma_i,\Sigma_j,P)

as the observed utility of applying operator i followed by j to problem class P.

Extend to formations:

K(
\Sigma_1,
\Sigma_2,
\ldots,
\Sigma_n
\mid P
)

This allows discovery of operator sequences such as:

\text{Mirror}
\rightarrow
\text{Thanatos}
\rightarrow
\text{Decrypto}

that outperform the same operators in another order.

Successful compositions may be promoted into new compound Seals.

⸻

8. Ratio Fossils

Some operator combinations behave differently depending on weighting.

Represent a formation as:

F=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
\alpha_i\Sigma_i

subject to:

\sum_i\alpha_i=1

The Metamap records high-performing weight vectors:

\rho^\*=
(
\alpha_1,
\alpha_2,
\ldots,
\alpha_n
)

These become Ratio Fossils.

Example:

0.7(\text{Garden})
+
0.3(\text{Reality})

may prove useful for exploratory ideation, while a different ratio works better for validation.

The ratio itself becomes part of the reusable strategy.

⸻

9. Failure Fossils

Failed routes are retained.

Define:

F^-=
(
Q,
\Pi,
\text{failure mode}
)

Common failure classes include:

* semantic collapse
* unsupported analogy
* excessive abstraction
* invariant loss
* circular reasoning
* excessive route depth
* high translation cost
* deceptive coherence
* premature convergence
* unproductive novelty

The Metamap uses these fossils as negative priors:

P(\Pi|Q,F^-)
\downarrow

Thus the system learns where not to travel.

⸻

10. Portal Desire-Lines

If many successful routes repeatedly use:

A\rightarrow B\rightarrow C

the Metamap identifies potential semantic demand for:

A\rightarrow C

Define portal desire:

D(A,C)
=
f(
\text{frequency},
\text{cost savings},
\text{loss reduction},
\text{utility}
)

If:

D(A,C)>\theta_D

the Portal Breeder may propose a direct transform:

T_{AC}

The proposed portal must pass:

1. invariant preservation testing,
2. round-trip reconstruction,
3. comparison against the existing route,
4. reality validation where appropriate.

This allows the semantic transportation network itself to evolve.

⸻

11. Seal Promotion

Repeated successful formations may be compressed.

If:

\Sigma_A
\rightarrow
\Sigma_B
\rightarrow
\Sigma_C

reliably acts as one functional unit, define:

\Sigma_{ABC}
=
\operatorname{Compress}
(
\Sigma_A,
\Sigma_B,
\Sigma_C
)

The new Seal stores:

* trigger conditions,
* operator sequence,
* invariants,
* ratio,
* portal preferences,
* failure modes,
* tests,
* stopping condition.

Thus experience can manufacture new operators.

⸻

12. Meta-Learning Objective

For strategy \Pi applied to situation Q, define:

U(\Pi|Q)
=
w_VV
+
w_\Delta\Delta
+
w_PP_I
+
w_NN
-
w_LL
-
w_CC
-
w_FF

where the weights reflect current mission priorities.

The Metamap seeks:

\Pi^\*
=
\arg\max_\Pi
\mathbb E[
U(\Pi|Q)
]

subject to minimum invariant preservation:

P_I\ge\tau_I

and bounded cost:

C\le B

